      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "$7\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\times 1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write $7\\,\\frac{1}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n7\\,\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{(7\\times 5)+1}{5}=\\frac{36}{5}\n$$\n\nWe also write  $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(1\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36}{5}\\times \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36}{5}\\times \\frac{5}{4} & =\\frac{36}{4}\\times \\frac{5}{5} \\\\  & =9\\times 1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\times 1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=9\n$$",
